Call me Lao Tse, to begin with. I come from a long (long!) line of Lao
Tses. We're monks by trade; that's what we write on all the official
forms, anyway. What we've really been doing, for generations, is
looking for some legendary hunk of junk. The "Amulet of Yendor," it's
called. I never believed in it, myself. My dad had gone off looking
for the silly thing when I was little, and never came back. The
official story in the family was that he must have met some tragic end
in the "Dungeons of Doom." (We're a little dramatic in my family.) Me,
I always assumed he just got tired of the family man thing, took off
for the hills, and was living like a king in Patagonia.

So I grew up with my mom, who was understandably bitter about the
whole adventuring thing, and my grandfather, who swore he'd actually
been in the Dungeons of Doom and brought back the Amulet. Of course,
anyone could tell that the one he had was just a cheap plastic
imitation. Grandpa used to regale us with tales of his adventures. No
one could tell what he'd really done and what he was making up off the
cuff. I mean, he was a really accomplished monk; he'd reached level
100, Venerable Old Koot, so he'd evidently really done some stuff. On
the other hand, he was the type of guy who liked to eat mushrooms and
mumble about something called the Borg, so who really knew what to
believe?

Anyway, I had to escape from all that drama every now and then. I used
to like to hang out in this wooded park called Ye Olde Forest (Tim
Benzadrine, proprietor). I was wandering around one day with my cat
Fluffy and happened upon a dead albino with some wheelbarrow tracks
leading away. Thinking that was odd, I leaned against a tree, happened
to press a knobby knot in the bark, and half the trunk caved in. The
tree was hollow, and there were stairs leading down underground. 

The Dungeons of Doom! So Gramps really had been there! It was just
like he'd described. I still wasn't sure about this silly amulet
thing, but when you have a long family tradition, and the opportunity
opens up (literally) to follow it, there's an irresistable compulsion
to do so. I descended the steps, grateful that I'd brought along some
food.

The dungeon seemed to be made of rooms with corridors connecting them.
Very few of the inhabitants seemed to be friendly. I had to start Kung
Fu-ing the natives when they attacked, and Fluffy did her part. I
started feeling more experienced and more confident of my abilities,
and Fluffy seemed to be getting bigger and stronger too. On the next
few levels I found some underground stores (seems like an odd place
for commerce, but then, it was an odd place in general)--a used armor
dealership, a tool shop, and a liquor emporium. The armor was pretty
much useless to me, but I got a few things from the tool shop, and a
few bottles of water from the liquor emporium. I didn't know what a
lot of the stuff was, and I didn't have enough money for most of it
anyway.

On the second level, I also found an altar. Hmm. Gramps must have
gotten here. He, like most of my family, is a devotee of that
wishy-washy god Chih Sung-tsu, and this was one of those gray altars.
I turned up my nose and went downstairs. Looking around, I found
another gray altar! I sighed, puzzled. Gramps wasn't that religious;
had more of my family been in this place? I was also irritated. I
mean, to each his own, but shouldn't a god stand for something? I
guess I was too much of a Boy Scout most of the time, but at least
Shan Lai Ching stood for law and order and stability, not just this,
"Hey, dude, just go with the flow" philosophy. I decided to sacrifice
the next monster I killed to Shan Lai Ching on that altar, just to
show them. The first time it didn't do anything, but the second time,
the altar glowed white. Cool!

In the same room was a downstairs. I poked my head down, heard some
dwarves singing some insipid ditty like "Heigh ho, heigh ho, it's off
to work we go!" and then some whistling. That place seemed too spooky,
so I kept going down the main dungeon. There was an oracle on the
fifth level, but she wouldn't answer any questions without a few
zorkmids in the palm. Wench. On the sixth level, I found another set
of stairs leading up into some place where I had to push a bunch of
boulders around in order to get anywhere. Occasionally I had to strip
down to my skivvies and squeeze into a crack in order to get where I
needed to go, but eventually I got through. I thought, "Hey, getting
this Amulet thingy isn't gonna be so hard after all. All I have to do
is to pick door number 1, 2, or 3." So I tried, but all I found was a
bag. Funny thing, though, stuff seemed to feel lighter when I put it
in the bag, so I kept it.

After exploring for a few more levels, I decided to go back and check
out that "Heigh ho" place. I sacrificed a few more times on the altar
I'd converted, and Shan Lai Ching granted me a long sword called
Snickersnee. Up to that point, I'd always felt swords were rather
cumbersome and liked fighting with my hands better, but hey, if my god
is going to give me stuff like this, I guess I'd better learn to use
it. Worked pretty well.

Took the stairs down again. Looked like a cave or some kind of mines
or something. Lotsa tools, and a gray rock that went "Scritch,
scritch" when you rubbed things on it. I found that I could use it to
tell the difference between real gems and worthless glass. A few
levels down, I fell down a hole, and ended up in kind of a town square
or something. There was a tool shop, a deli, a general store, and a
lighting shop with a proprietor who liked to chat about coffee and
some sports franchise I'd never heard of called the "devteam." There
was a temple there dedicated to Shan Lai Ching. I did some more
sacrificing there, and received another sword called Frost Brand. Now
that was a cool blade! I also prayed on the altar, and got some water
blessed. A little trick Gramps had taught me.

The level below the town, I received the shock of my life. I was
looking around, when I thought I saw a ghost. It was muttering
something about murder most foul, as in the best it is;
but this most foul, strange and unnatural. I looked at the tombstone.
It read, "Here lies Lao Tse, killed by a gnome king." It was my
father! I thought it most humane in the long run to put his ghost out
of its misery, and then it seemed a waste not to take what I could of
his stuff. Some scrolls, potions, wands, and a rather shiny amulet.
Too bad it didn't look anything like the cheap plastic imitation that
Gramps had, or I'd have been out of that dungeon quick. By this time I
had some scrolls of identify and it ID'd as reflection, which seemed
useful, so I put it on. There was also a different gray rock, which
didn't look like much, but the general store shopkeeper seemed to
think it was worth something, so I kept it. I didn't think it was
worth it to go down further into the mines; I'd already stepped into a
trap and became a gnome mummy for a while. I didn't want to repeat
that experience.

One irritating thing was that, even though I had all these gems, only
one shopkeeper was even interested in them. I got a couple thousand
zorkmids from him, and received some protection from the minetown
priest, but I couldn't sell the rest to buy more protection. If I
wasn't such a boy scout, I would have roughed up the other
shopkeepers, but... 

Back in the normal dungeon ("normal"?), I kept exploring. When I got
down to level 12, I heard The Grand Master (the director of the Monk
camp that my mom and Gramps had made me go to) calling for help. Oh
crap. I'm in the middle of one adventure I wasn't looking for, and he
wants to suck me into another one. I tried to ignore it and kept on
exploring.

I stashed some of the stuff I'd found in a spare bag on dungeon level
14. On that same level, I encountered an ice troll that WOULD NOT STAY
DEAD. I must have killed that thing 15 times, but every time, it would
rise back up and keep coming at me, chanting various arcane
incantations such as, "All of You Idiots Make Me Want to Vomit in
RAGE" and "I'll top-post if I damn well please!" and "I ascended in
less than 9000 turns with an AC of -2!" Stoopid trolls.

On the next level, I met a rather friendly nurse (her name was
Margaret Hoolihan) and we did a little dancing...

Emerging from the downstairs to dlvl 21, I was confronted by another
ghost of one of my ancestors. The Phantom slowly, gravely, silently
approached. When it came, I bent down upon one knee; for in the very
air through which this Spirit moved it seemed to scatter gloom and
mystery. It was shrouded in a deep black garment, which concealed its
head, its face, its form, and left nothing of it visible save one
outstretched hand. But for this it would have been difficult to detach
its figure from the night, and separate it from the darkness by which
it was surrounded. I whacked it and grabbed for its stash. Later on
the same level I encountered another ghost! It was muttering to
itself, "I should have listened. He told me, 'Lao Tse, you're my older
brother, and I love you. But don't ever take sides with anyone against
the Family again. Ever.'" In his stash, I found a helmet that made me
feel really smart and wise, and a wand that gave me a wish when I
test-engraved it. I ended up wishing for Magicbane; Gramps had told me
that it was good for resisting spells and curses if you got in a tight
spot. Between the stashes, shops, etc., I had a nice collection of
spellbooks, which I started blessing, reading, and polymorphing with a
wand I'd found, and I began to get a nice collection of spells. I
practiced the easier ones, to get better at the tougher ones. The ones
I found most useful were magic missile, fireball, magic mapping, and
identify.

Somewhere around this time, I made a bad mistake. I bit into a black
pudding, thinking that eating blobs and jellies had been okay, but
when I tasted the blood trickling into my mouth, I felt grossed out
and guilty. I promised myself that I wouldn't do that again, and never
did, except when I forgot and ate a wraith corpse once.

Down on dlvl 25, I suddenly met up with a master lich! This was not a
fun encounter. He disintegrated my robe, the perv! I finally whacked
him into dust, but scurried up to dlvl 21 and grabbed another one from
one of my dearly departed ancestors.

By this point, I was beginning to feel that I ought to help out The
Grand Master after all, so I went back and jumped into the magic
portal I'd found. I found myself at my old dilapidated campground, the
Monastery of Chan Sune. To be honest, I didn't notice much difference,
but The Grand Master insisted that things had really gone downhill
since his rival camp director Master Kaen had come to town and made
off with a set of spiffy shades called the Eyes of the Overworld. I
had thought that they were just some John Lennon granny glasses that
The Grand Master liked to wear as an affectation, but he seemed pretty
bent on getting them back, so I obliged him by searching for this Kaen
fellow. I figured I'd make him an offer he couldn't refuse. I tried
wishing for a wand of death to rub him out quickly, but there must
have been a glitch in the space time continuum, because all I got was
a wand of sleep. So I decided, what the heck, I think I'm tough enough
to take him. I found the Monastery of the Earth-Lord (a much more
upscale campground than the one my mother could afford), made my way
down to Master Kaen's lair, and as it turned out, when I tried to zap
him with the wand of sleep, I ended up missing him but waking him up.
Luckily, I was close to the upstairs, so I backed up there and whacked
at him with Frost Brand. He ended up going down, and I breathed a big
sigh of relief.

By this time I'd discovered quite a number of potions, and mixed them
together according to the formulas Gramps had taught me, along with
how to select the best mushrooms. Several golden potions quaffed, and
several more stashed away for emergencies, had me feeling a little
invincible. I also found that reading scrolls of charging made me
feel, well, kind of charged up when accompanied by some of the White
Lightnin' I'd found.

The Grand Master let me keep the Eyes for a while (as usual, he was
absorbed in a chess game at the time), and I went back down to tackle
the Castle. I cleared out the whole bloody thing, but all the armor
and other items were useless. Found a wand of wishing, and wished for
some Gauntlets of Dexterity. After looking around a little more and
finding neither a downstairs nor the Amulet I was after, I decided to
jump through a hole I'd levitated over before.

The odor of burnt flesh and decay pervaded the air. I heard groans and
moans everywhere. The level was filled with a strange sort of fire, as
one great Furnace flam'd, yet from those flames no light, but rather
darkness visible serv'd only to discover sights of woe. Oh hell, I
thought. The rest of the levels were too horrid and mind numbing to
recount. Suffice to say, most of the time I felt like a rat in a maze.
Magic mapping was helpful, although I found it draining, and ended up
reading some more scrolls of charging while a bit tipsy. 

I've heard that demon lords sometimes let adventurers bribe their way
past, but I had no such luck. My most serious encounter involved
Orcus, who disappeared for a while and reappeared with Yeenoghu. I
lured them down a level and used Bonetti's Defense against them on the
stairs. On the way down from there, I used the telepathy that Shan Lai
Ching had given me and recognized the Wizard of Yendor in the center
of one of the levels. Further on down, I found a portal on an island,
and had a pretty good idea that somehow it would lead me to the
Wizard. He must have the Amulet, I thought. Down on level 48, I came
upon a wobbly tile in the floor. The landlord should really keep this
place up better, I thought, and marked it with a ruby marked with the
phrase "Vibrating Square," and left directions to it on the
downstairs.

Making my way back up the levels, I found an upstairs leading to a
tower. All I found there was a pale, wimpy fellow named Vlad and a
candelabrum, which made a nice setting for the candles I'd bought from
the Columbian supremo devotee. By this time I had a pretty good idea
of what I had to do, so I levelported back up to my stashes in the
main dungeon and got things ready so that when I pinched the Amulet, I
could make a clean getaway.

I fought my way up the Wizard's tower, resting after clearing out each
level to recharge my mana. Finally on the top level, I zapped a wand
of cold to freeze the moat, and breathed an incantation to myself:
"Hello, my name is Lao Tse. You killed my father. Prepare to die." A
wand of digging to get into the tower, and a wand of death to snuff
out the Wizard. "I want my father back, you son of a bitch," I said,
as I rushed in to grab the Amu--

A book? A stupid Papyrus book? Where was the Amulet? Bloody hell,
Gramps, what did you get me into this for? I pulled out a cursed
scroll of teleport and levelported down to level 48. I found the
vibrating square again, and on a hunch, lit the Candelabrum. I rang a
silver bell I'd gotten from Master Kaen, thinking it would keep the
nasties away for a while while I read this silly book. Maybe it would
give me a clue to where the Amulet was, or if there even was one. I
had the feeling that it was all just an absurd legend.

Suddenly a moat and some fire traps opened up all around me, and a
staircase opened below. A secret passage. Maybe I'll end up in the
Conservatory, I thought to myself as I descended.

I tried to use magic mapping on this level, but just got dizzy, so I
started looking around. I found a graveyard full of sleeping ugly
critters, and whacked a path through them. I used the Bell as a
trap-and-secret-door detector and wands of teleportation to get
nasties out of my way. Finding my way to the door into Moloch's
Sanctum, I got a glimpse of the High Priestess. She was shaved bald,
and was ripping up a photo of the Pope. Thinking that was rude, I
zapped her with my handy dandy wand of death. When I got to her, I
pried from her cold, dead fingers the Amulet of Yendor! I recognized
it as the real thing by the absence of the "Made in China" stamp on
the back. I quickly drank a cursed potion of gain level in order to
get out of there quick.

Going up levels proved difficult in Gehennom, as some mysterious force
kept knocking me back a few levels. The Wizard of Yendor, whom I
taunted by calling him "Rodney" (he hated that) kept reappearing, and
I kept having to zap him. The scariest time was when he reappeared in
Orcustown, catching me with a used-up wand of death. He
double-troubled me and summoned an elf-lord to fight me. I whacked at
one of the Rodneys with Frostbrand, and when he disappeared to heal
up, I cast "charm monster," making the elf-lord my pet. I quickly
charged up the WoD, and when Rodney came back, got him in two zaps. I
think I turned a little green when the elf-lord ate the Wizard of
Yendor's corpse. Ugh.

Once I got out of Gehennom, the rest proved easy. I had food set aside
in my stashes, and made sure I had wands of teleportation, wands of
digging, and a lovely crystal ball I'd found early in my travels. I
thought I'd just escape the dungeon, go home, and show off the Amulet
to Gramps. That would irritate him! I quaffed one last cursed potion
of gain level, just to escape in style, but found myself not in Ye
Olde Forest, but on the Plane of Earth. I guess it's true: you can't
go home again.

The crystal ball showed me where portals to each next plane were.
Wands of digging got me through Earth. Air wasn't as hard as I'd
heard; I think my armor, protection, and heavy use of golden potions
had fortified me for air elementals, and I teleported them away
sometimes. A ring of levitation and a wand of cold (just in case) got
me through Fire. The portal on Water was revealed by my crystal ball,
but then disappeared, and I wondered how long it would take to find it
again. Turned out it revealed itself once I was in the right bubble.

A guardian angel greeted me on the Astral plane. "He's going to guard
me?" I thought. I moved toward the right, and used teleport and
jumping to get quickly to the altar. It was the right one--I mean, you
already knew it was the right one, not the center or the left, but it
was right; I mean, correct--and I offered the Amulet of Yendor to Shan
Lai Ching... An invisible choir sang (I think it was the na-nas from
"Hey Jude"), and I was bathed in radience, like a most excellent
tanning salon... The voice of Shan Lai Ching rang out:
"Congratulations, mortal. In return for thy service, I grant thee the
gift of immortality!" I ascended to the status of Demigod, and the
rest is history...

